From 51c3d320830c2fe825e6c2256c3027b590fe2765 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 09:27:04 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] drm: Test a refused output configuration again with linear
 buffers

Some layouts are scanned out only from linear buffers. amdgpu shows
DisplayPort's frame sequential stereo only from a linear, single-plane
RGB framebuffer (edition kernel series 39): the right eye is the left
one moved by an eye's width, a plain byte offset in a linear buffer
but not across the blocks of a tiled or compressed one. KWin's probe of
the mode passes, because it tests with a dumb buffer, which is linear,
but the renderer then picks a tiled buffer for the output, and the
configuration with the mode is refused.

When a configuration fails with the buffers the renderer chose and
then with the low bandwidth formats, it is now tested again with only
linear buffers on the primary plane: on each enabled output on its own
first, so that the others keep their buffers, then on all of them. An
output keeps linear buffers for as long as it keeps that mode; a new
mode is tested with the renderer's choice first. A surface made linear
for such a configuration gives way to the renderer's choice again when
other modifiers are allowed.

Configurations that pass today never reach the new tests, so nothing
changes for them, and when no linear buffer passes either, the
configuration is refused as before.

testMockDrmDisplayPortSequential::testLinearFallback gives the mock
planes IN_FORMATS with a tiled and the linear modifier and makes the
mock refuse frame sequential stereo from anything but a linear
framebuffer, as amdgpu does. The twin of a 120 Hz mode is accepted with
a linear buffer on its output while the other output keeps its tiled
one, the 2D mode gets the tiled buffer back, and without a linear
modifier on the plane the mode stays refused.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5
